= Planning (artificial intelligence) — Technical =

== State-transition formulation ==
A deterministic planning model can be represented by:

<pre>
S = states
A = actions
γ(s,a) = transition
s_0 = initial state
G = goal condition
</pre>

A plan is an action sequence:

<pre>
π = ⟨a_1, ..., a_n⟩
</pre>

whose induced trajectory reaches a state satisfying G.

== Beyond classical planning ==
Planning models can incorporate:

* nondeterministic effects;
* probabilistic transitions;
* partial observability;
* temporal actions;
* resource constraints;
* hierarchical task decomposition;
* multiple agents;
* continuous state/action spaces.

== Planning and acting ==
Execution introduces observation error, unexpected events, model mismatch, and changing objectives.

Continual deliberation may alternate:

<pre>
observe → plan/refine → act → monitor → replan
</pre>

